---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../config.js';
import { rates, appliances } from '../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/seo';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Methodology', href: '/methodology/' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={`How ${SITE_NAME} Calculates Costs: Data Sources & Methodology`} description={`Where our electricity rates come from, how appliance wattages and usage patterns are chosen, and the exact formulas behind every number on ${SITE_NAME}.`} schema={[breadcrumbSchema(crumbs)]}>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-6 pb-10">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink-900 dark:text-ink-100">How We Calculate Costs</h1>
    <div class="mt-6 prose-custom">
      <p>
        Every number on {SITE_NAME} is computed from two inputs: <strong>current state-level electricity
        rates</strong> and <strong>realistic appliance power data</strong>. This page documents both, plus the
        formulas connecting them, so you can judge — and adjust — every estimate we publish.
      </p>

      <h2>Electricity rates</h2>
      <p>
        We use the <strong>average residential retail electricity price per state</strong> published by the
        U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA), currently reflecting <strong>{rates.periodLabel}</strong> data.
        Rates are refreshed monthly as the EIA publishes new figures. The current national average in our
        dataset is {rates.national.cents}¢/kWh.
      </p>
      <p>
        A state average is exactly that — your utility, plan, and tier may differ, and your <em>effective</em>
        rate (bill total ÷ kWh, including delivery charges and taxes) is typically somewhat higher than
        nominal supply rates. For maximum accuracy, compute your effective rate from a recent bill and use it
        in our <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a>, which accepts any rate via the state selector's underlying math.
      </p>

      <h2>Appliance power data</h2>
      <p>
        Our database covers {appliances.length} appliances. For each we publish a <strong>typical wattage</strong> and
        a realistic range, drawn from U.S. Department of Energy and ENERGY STAR published figures, manufacturer
        specifications, and the physics of the device class (for example, resistance heaters converting
        electricity to heat 1:1). Two usage models are used:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Continuous devices</strong> (heaters, TVs, fridges): typical watts × typical hours per day. For
        cycling devices like refrigerators, the wattage shown is an <em>effective average</em> that already accounts
        for compressor duty cycle.</li>
        <li><strong>Session devices</strong> (dishwashers, dryers, EV charging): energy per use (kWh) × uses per week.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        "Typical usage" assumptions are stated on every appliance page and are deliberately ordinary — an
        8-hour winter day for a space heater, five dryer loads a week for a family. Your habits will differ;
        that's why every page embeds a calculator with adjustable inputs.
      </p>

      <h2>The formulas</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>kWh/day = watts ÷ 1,000 × hours/day (continuous) or kWh/use × uses/week ÷ 7 (session)</li>
        <li>Cost/day = kWh/day × rate</li>
        <li>Cost/month = cost/day × 30.4 (average month length)</li>
        <li>Cost/year = cost/day × 365</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Where standby power is meaningful, kWh/day adds standby watts × non-active hours. Monthly figures for
        seasonal appliances (AC, holiday lights) describe an <em>in-season</em> month, noted on those pages.
      </p>

      <h2>What our numbers are — and aren't</h2>
      <p>
        These are planning estimates, not measurements of your specific devices, and not billing predictions.
        They're built to be honest at the "which of these is big?" level that drives good decisions. For your
        own exact figures, a plug-in energy meter is cheap and definitive — we explain the method in
        <a href="/guides/how-to-measure-appliance-power/">How to Measure What Any Appliance Really Uses</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If you believe a figure is wrong, we want to know. Reach us via the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> —
        corrections are reviewed against primary sources and published promptly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
